  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Nijū-kyū (Japanese 29). Logic: See rules.objective and rules.gameplay.',
    Player: "Nijū-kyū (Twenty-Nine) is a tactical Japanese card game focused on cumulative addition and card capture. Played with a standard deck, the objective is to be the player who plays a card that brings the total on the table exactly to twenty-nine. The player who hits the target 'sweeps' the table and collects the cards as scoring points. It is a game of subtraction-logic, card counting, and opportunistic 'sniping' of the target total.",
    objective: 'Capture the most cards by hitting the cumulative total of 29.',
    gameplay: '1. Players take turns playing one card face-up to the table.\n2. The total sum of point values is announced.\n3. **Hitting 29**: If your card makes the total exactly 29, you win all cards on the table and keep them in your score pile.\n4. **Passing**: If you only have cards that would push the total OVER 29, you must pass your turn.\n5. **Reset**: After a sweep (hitting 29), the next player starts a new pile from zero.',
    keyRules: [
      'Card values: 2-10 equal face value; A, J, Q, K count as 1 point each.',
      'Playing a card that makes the running total exactly 29 sweeps all table cards to your score pile.',
      'If you have no card that can be played without exceeding 29, you must pass.',
      'After a sweep, the next player starts a new pile from zero.',
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: false,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
